# SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-3.0-or-later
# Copyright (C) 2026 Jeff Francis
#
# sage040.specs - what `gcc hello.c -o hello` has to mean on this
# system.
#
# Until now every program here was linked with eleven explicit flags
# (libc/libc.mk, ports/cross.sh): -nostdlib, a linker script, a start
# file, a library group. That is workable when a Makefile in this tree
# is doing the linking. It is not workable when the thing doing the
# linking is somebody at a prompt on the machine, or a configure
# script, which writes `${CC} -o conftest ${CFLAGS} ${LDFLAGS}
# conftest.c` and nothing else -- binutils' own sanity check does
# exactly that, and failed on it.
#
# So the knowledge moves into the compiler, which is where it belongs.
#
#   gcc hello.c -o hello            a dynamic program, against
#                                   /lib/libc.so through /lib/ld.so
#   gcc -static hello.c -o hello    a static one, laid out by
#                                   sage040.ld, which runs on a disk
#                                   with no libraries on it at all
#
# Dynamic is the default because that is what Linux does and what the
# machine is set up for: ld.so and libc.so are in /lib on every disk
# this system builds.
#
# WHY EACH LINE IS WHAT IT IS:
#
# startfile  crt0.o for a static program, crt0-dyn.o for a dynamic
#            one. They differ: the dynamic one has the constructor
#            walk the arrays the stock linker script brackets, and
#            the static one uses sage040.ld's own symbols.
# endfile    empty. crtend.o belongs to a hosted GCC runtime this
#            system does not have; the .fini_array walk is in exit().
# lib        picolibc's libc, and liblinux -- the system call layer --
#            in a group with it, because the two call each other and
#            a single pass cannot resolve that. A dynamic program
#            needs no group: libc.so has the lot in one object.
# link       -Bstatic and -shared are spelled out because THIS GCC'S
#            DRIVER PASSES NEITHER TO THE LINKER: `-static -lc` with
#            libc.so present quietly produced a DYNAMIC program asking
#            for /usr/lib/libc.so.1.
#            --build-id=none because nothing here reads a build id and
#            it costs a note section in every program.
#            The dynamic layout is the one ld.so expects: text at
#            0x10000000, eager binding (ld.so binds eagerly anyway),
#            and SysV hashes, which are what ld.so reads.

*startfile:
%{static:crt0%O%s}%{!static:crt0-dyn%O%s}

*endfile:


*lib:
%{static:--start-group -lc -llinux --end-group}%{!static:-lc}

*link:
--build-id=none %{static:-Bstatic -T sage040.ld%s --no-warn-rwx-segments}%{!static:-Ttext-segment=0x10000000 --dynamic-linker=/lib/ld.so -z now --hash-style=sysv}

